          "course_id": "c1",
          "title": "Mitosis in short",
          "body": "Mitosis is the process by which one eukaryotic cell divides to make two genetically identical daughter cells.\n\nThe four phases\n• Prophase: chromosomes condense and become visible; the nuclear envelope breaks down.\n• Metaphase: chromosomes line up at the equator of the cell; spindle fibres attach to the centromeres.\n• Anaphase: sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles; each chromatid becomes a separate chromosome.\n• Telophase: new nuclear envelopes form around each set of chromosomes; chromosomes unwind.\n\nAfter mitosis\nCytokinesis divides the cytoplasm, giving two separate daughter cells.\n\nWhy it matters\nGrowth and development, replacing old or damaged cells (such as skin cells), healing after injury, and asexual reproduction in single-celled organisms."
